raw:
{
  "slots": {
    "question": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper investigates whether the use of stigmatizing labels like 'convicted criminal' in Democratic campaign advertisements against Trump caused spillover effects that negatively impacted Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records and their sense of political representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "this study explores what may have been the impact of propagating such labels on Democrats’ perceptions of people with criminal records, and to what extent does the propagation of such stigmatizing terminology impact how well Democrats perceive to be represented by their own elected officials?",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "To test for the possibility of stigmatizing language spillover, this preliminary study employs a survey experiment testing exposure to the Party’s advertisement, labeling Trump as a “convicted criminal” (n = 814) relative to no advertisement (n = 813), on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records and several dimensions of perceived representation.",
            "page": 1
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study also asks whether providing a counter-narrative featuring a formerly incarcerated state representative can mitigate the negative effects of the stigmatizing advertisement.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Additionally, this study seeks to understand whether providing a counterexample to Trump – highlighting the perspective of a formerly system-involved representative on the propagation of this label by the Democratic party – may counter-act potential negative impacts of the Party’s Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 4
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "central_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to the Democratic advertisement labeling Trump as a 'convicted criminal' significantly worsened Democrats' perceptions of individuals with criminal records, but these negative effects were almost entirely ameliorated when participants were first exposed to a counter-narrative from a formerly incarcerated legislator.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Results suggest that exposure to the Trump advertisement with the “convicted criminal” label had significant, negative impacts on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with records, but improved perceived feelings of representation. Importantly, the effect of a counter-stereotype’s ability to mitigate these effects was also tested... Findings suggest that the counter-stereotype vastly ameliorated the negative effects of the advertisement on Democrats’ perceptions of individuals with criminal records.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "First, there is clear evidence that the Trump advertisement caused decreases in positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records amongst Democrats, but when paired with (and preceded by) a counter-narrative of a dedicated, system-impacted state representative who speaks to the harm of the “criminal” label, these effects were almost entirely ameliorated",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "evidence": [
      {
        "claim": "The study utilizes a survey experiment conducted via Prolific with 2,439 self-identified Democrats, randomly assigned to three groups: Control, Trump Ad Alone, and Counter-Narrative First.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Thus, the 2,439 participants were allocated approximately evenly across one of three different treatment groups: a) the Control group (n = 813); b) the Trump Ad Alone group (n = 814); and c) the Counter-Narrative First group (n = 812).",
            "page": 4
          },
          {
            "text": "The survey was fielded through Prolific, an online survey platform that is commonly utilized for conducting survey-based research, which has been demonstrated as a reliable platform for obtaining high-quality data. 2,500 participants were recruited for this study, between July 12th and 13th of 2025",
            "page": 8
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The experimental stimuli included an image of Trump's mugshot labeled 'CONVICTED CRIMINAL' and a text excerpt from an article about Jovan Jackson, a formerly incarcerated Nevada assemblymember, serving as the counter-narrative.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Participants were presented with one of the images utilized in advertisements within the 2024 presidential election, specifically President Donald J. Trump’s mugshot, which was labeled, in all capital letters, “CONVICTED CRIMINAL.”",
            "page": 5
          },
          {
            "text": "This excerpt was from the article entitled, “Could a formerly incarcerated lawmaker reshape justice policy in the NV Legislature?,” by Michael Lyle, published in the Nevada Current on November 5, 2024 [55].",
            "page": 5
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Outcomes were measured using indices for perceptions of individuals with criminal records, quality of Democratic representation, and motives of elected officials, analyzed via least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard errors.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "The primary outcomes of interest in this study were: a) perceptions of individuals with criminal records; b) perceived value of representation; c) perceived quality of Democratic representation in federal government; d) complexity of representation; e) motives of elected officials; and f) advertisement support.",
            "page": 7
          },
          {
            "text": "To estimate each ATE, least squares regressions with heteroskedasticity-consistent standard error estimators, HC-2, were utilized.",
            "page": 10
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "key_findings": [
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records among Democrats by more than 0.8 standard deviation units compared to the control group.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement alone significantly decreased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records ( β̂0 = −0.835, SE = 0.044)",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Exposure to the counter-narrative prior to the advertisement significantly increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation units relative to seeing the advertisement alone, effectively neutralizing the ad's negative impact.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "exposure to the counter-narrative prior to being presented with the Trump advertisement increased positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records by more than 0.8 standard deviation (SD) units, compared to those presented with the Trump advertisement alone ( β̂2 = 0.83, SE = 0.041).",
            "page": 11
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "While the Trump advertisement improved perceived quality of Democratic representation and feelings of being represented, the counter-narrative significantly decreased the perceived quality of Democratic representation compared to the advertisement alone.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "evidence supported H1D, namely that exposure to the counter-narrative significantly decreased quality of Democratic representation in federal government, relative to just Trump advertisement exposure ( β̂2 = −0.332, SE = 0.048).",
            "page": 11
          },
          {
            "text": "exposure to the Trump advertisement significantly increased feelings of being well represented by elected officials and the extent to which Democrats perceived that elected officials were motivated by public service over self-interest.",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "System-impacted individuals reported significantly more positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records than non-system-impacted individuals, and the negative effects of the advertisement were more damaging for system-impacted participants.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "those who identified as being system-impacted reported significantly more positive perceptions overall and within each treatment arm, with only one exception.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "the Trump advertisement exposure appeared to be significantly more damaging to perceptions of individuals with criminal records for those who are system-impacted.",
            "page": 14
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "Racial attitudes were a strong predictor of outcomes, with more positive racial attitudes associated with more positive perceptions of individuals with criminal records and higher quality of representation.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Most strongly and consistently predictive of nearly all outcomes, with exception of the public service motivation of elected officials, was this sample of Democrats’ racial attitudes.",
            "page": 13
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "scope_and_limits": [
      {
        "claim": "The study lacks a full factorial design, meaning it cannot isolate the specific effect of the counter-narrative alone without the advertisement, nor can it disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing text.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "failing to include the counter-narrative only arm limits the ability to interpret the additional impact of including it with the advertisements; in other words, the counter-narrative arm cannot be directly compared to the pure control arm to test the impact of the counter-narrative alone because it also involves exposure to the Trump advertisement.",
            "page": 16
          },
          {
            "text": "as the advertisement (i.e., Trump’s mugshot with the words ‘convicted criminal’ imposed on the image) was presented consistently across treatment arms, it is not possible to disentangle the impact of the image from the stigmatizing terminology imposed",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The results are based on a single time-point survey fielded after the election, so the durability of the effects and their applicability during active campaign periods or in real-world voting behavior remain unclear.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Specifically, the single time-point survey experimental approach is a useful first step, but leaves unanswered several key questions related to the ability of these results to replicate: a) for how long do the effects of the counter-narrative prime before the advertisement and the advertisement itself persist;",
            "page": 17
          },
          {
            "text": "Whether these results replicate during campaign periods is an open question, perhaps especially so for the advertisement’s impacts in particular.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "There are concerns regarding the potential influence of generative AI agents on online survey responses, although the author notes Prolific has lower prevalence compared to other platforms.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "It is also incredibly important to acknowledge that this survey was fielded amongst an online panel of survey respondents, and the proliferation of generative AI should cause concerns over the humanity of the responses provided within the survey.",
            "page": 17
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "theoretical_positioning": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper positions itself within social categorization theory, arguing that the salience of the 'convicted criminal' category applied to Trump leads to spillover effects on perceptions of the broader group of individuals with criminal records.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Leaning upon social categorization theory, propagating such labels on Trump may thereby spillover, impacting constituents’ perspectives of individuals with records more broadly.",
            "page": 1
          },
          {
            "text": "Specifically, social categorization theory articulates the implications and process of how individuals come to assign and distinguish individuals into groups, with much of the theory and evidence pointing to the importance of category salience and the role of group exemplars and prototypes",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "claim": "The study extends the literature on perspective-getting and counter-stereotypes by applying them to the context of political messaging and criminal justice stigma, building on prior evidence that these interventions increase empathy and positive perceptions.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "Alternatively, one presumably unexplored messaging intervention in the 2024 election – though heavily supported by political science and psychological literature – is perspective-getting [42–44]. Perspective-getting, or being provided with the perspective of another, has been shown to significantly increase feelings of empathy for members of an out-group",
            "page": 3
          },
          {
            "text": "the strength of the counter-narrative is not unexpected, but promising, given prior evidence on the effects of perspective-getting and counter-stereotypical narratives on increasing empathy for members of an outgroup",
            "page": 15
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "interlocutor_claim": [
      {
        "claim": "The paper disputes the strategic utility of negative campaigning for election success, citing meta-analytic evidence that shows such ads do not empirically aid election success, while noting that Democrats tend to respond more to positive ads.",
        "quotes": [
          {
            "text": "meta-analytic evidence does not empirically support the use of negative campaign advertisements to aid election success [7].",
            "page": 2
          },
          {
            "text": "prior evidence on the effects of negative campaign advertisements suggests that they tend to be utilized less by those leading in the polls [4,5] and that Democrats tend to respond more to positive ads, compared to Republicans [6].",
            "page": 2
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
